  "explanation": "The proposal introduces a creative synthesis by combining multi-agent scientific ideation loops with latent-space novelty probing adapted from recent work like Think-Probe-Respond. While multi-agent ideation and retrieval-based novelty checking have been explored separately, adapting hidden-state probing to dynamically steer agent dialogues away from medium-novelty biases is a compelling direction. However, the conceptual overlap with existing latent probing for novelty judgment and multi-agent RAG frameworks makes the incremental contribution moderate rather than groundbreaking.",
  "problems": [
    "The adaptation of latent-space probes from a single-model judgment task (Think-Probe-Respond) to a multi-turn, multi-agent interactive dialogue loop introduces significant compounding error and representation drift.",
    "Probing hidden states of quantized 8B models (e.g., via 4-bit loading on a 12GB GPU) may degrade the fidelity of the latent representations required for reliable linear probing of abstract scientific novelty.",
    "The proposal lacks sufficient detail on how the discrete trigger derived from the probe mapping will reliably modify structural prompts or contrastive constraints without causing semantic incoherence in the generator agent."
  ],
  "improvements": [
    "Provide a pilot validation demonstrating that linear probes trained on hidden states of a 4-bit quantized 8B model maintain stability across multi-turn conversational exchanges.",
    "Clarify the exact mapping function from probe activation entropy and continuous calibration thresholds to the proposed prompt template fallbacks.",
    "Include a baseline comparison against recent prompting-based mitigation strategies and explicit post-hoc filtering methods to isolate the exact gains provided by the latent probe."
  ],
